// pgpool.nv — a connection pool as a VALUE with a POLICY, and the
// three things a pool has to do that a list of sockets does not.
//
// A POOL IS A POLICY, AND THE POLICY IS THE PART WORTH PUBLISHING.
// Keeping sockets in a list is the easy half and nobody gets it
// wrong.  What a pool is actually for is three decisions, each of
// which is a wrong answer waiting to happen:
//
//   1. WHEN TO OPEN ANOTHER.  A pool that opens on demand up to a
//      limit converts a traffic spike into a connection storm, and a
//      PostgreSQL server's cost per connection is a process.  The
//      limit is therefore not advice — past it, `acquire` WAITS or
//      REFUSES, and which one is `PgPoolPolicy.wait_ms`.
//   2. WHETHER A RETURNED CONNECTION IS STILL GOOD.  This is the one
//      that produces the famous bug: a connection returned in the
//      middle of a transaction hands the NEXT borrower an open
//      transaction, and that borrower's first statement runs inside
//      somebody else's.  `release` therefore checks
//      `pgmsg.PgReadyForQuery`'s status and rolls back rather than
//      trusting the borrower — see `reset_on_release`.
//   3. WHEN TO THROW ONE AWAY.  An idle connection can be closed by
//      the server, by a firewall, or by `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`,
//      and none of those tells the client.  So a connection past
//      `max_idle_ms` is closed rather than lent, and one that has
//      served `max_uses` is recycled — which is also what bounds the
//      memory a long-lived backend accumulates from prepared
//      statements.
//
// THE POOL IS A VALUE AND EVERY OPERATION ANSWERS A NEW ONE, which
// means it does not synchronise anything and cannot: two tasks
// sharing one pool share it the way they share any other value in
// this language, which is to say through whatever the program uses
// for that.  Saying so is better than a lock this package cannot make
// correct — `cell.pool` is how novo-lang programs own shared state,
// and a pool per cell with no sharing at all is the shape that
// actually runs.  The README says this rather than leaving a reader
// to find out.
//
// TWO CONSEQUENCES OF BEING A VALUE ARE WRITTEN DOWN HERE RATHER THAN
// LEFT FOR A CALLER TO DISCOVER:
//
//   * NOTHING CAN CHANGE A POOL WHILE `acquire` IS INSIDE IT.  There
//     is no other writer to wait for — a wait would be a loop against
//     a clock that changes nothing — so `acquire` REFUSES as soon as
//     the limit is reached, whatever `wait_ms` says, and `wait_ms` is
//     carried for the caller that owns the pool through a `cell` and
//     retries on its own deadline, which is the only place a wait can
//     be correct.
//   * AN `Err` CARRIES NO POOL.  A refusal therefore has nowhere to be
//     recorded: `PgPoolStats.timeouts_total` stays zero for a pool
//     this package built, and the refusal is the `PgPoolExhausted` in
//     the error.  A caller that reports on timeouts counts them where
//     it sees the error; one that wants them in the pool's own
//     counter cannot have them there — see `stats`.
//
// THE USE COUNT RIDES A LEDGER RATHER THAN THE CONNECTION, because a
// lent connection is out of the idle list and there is nowhere inside
// a `PgConn` to keep a number.  Each lend records the backend pid the
// handshake gave that connection and the count it went out with, and
// the release that matches the pid gives the count back — which is
// what makes `max_uses` an exact number of lends rather than a guess
// at one.  See `lent_pids`.
//
// `[io, net, time]` IS DECLARED because every one of the three
// decisions above is about a clock — an idle age, a lifetime, a wait
// deadline — and because every connection in the pool is a socket
// this package writes to.  `now_ms` is the CALLER's reading wherever
// the API takes one, so a test can age a pool without sleeping and a
// program with one clock read per request does not take another here;
// `warm` and `reap` take their own and say so.

use pgconn
use pgerror
use pgmsg
use pgquery

// What the pool is allowed to do.
pub struct PgPoolPolicy
    // The most connections this pool will ever have open.  Not
    // advice: past it `acquire` waits or refuses.
    max_connections: Int
    // The fewest to keep open when idle.  Zero is right for a program
    // that runs occasionally; a server wants enough to absorb a burst
    // without a handshake on the critical path.
    min_connections: Int
    // How long a pool WOULD wait for one to come back.  This package
    // refuses instead — a value pool has no other writer to wait for,
    // see the module comment — and the number is kept because a
    // policy is also a configuration a caller reports on, and because
    // a caller that owns its pool through a `cell` uses it as the
    // deadline for its own retry.  ZERO MEANS REFUSE IMMEDIATELY,
    // which is the right answer for a request handler with its own
    // deadline — a pool that waits forever turns a slow query into a
    // stalled service.
    wait_ms: Int
    // Close an idle connection older than this.  Guards against the
    // server, a firewall or an idle timeout having closed it without
    // telling anyone.  Zero means never close one for age.
    max_idle_ms: Int
    // Recycle a connection that has served this many times.  What
    // bounds the memory a long-lived backend accumulates from
    // prepared statements and temporary tables.  Zero means never.
    max_uses: Int
    // Roll back and reset a connection as it comes back, rather than
    // trusting the borrower to have left it clean.  True by default,
    // and the reason is in the module comment.
    reset_on_release: Bool
    // Run this SQL on every freshly opened connection — a
    // `SET search_path`, a `SET statement_timeout`.  Empty for none.
    // A statement that fails closes the connection and fails the call
    // that opened it: a pool whose `search_path` could not be set is
    // a pool whose every later statement runs somewhere else.
    on_connect_sql: Str

// A pool.
//
// A VALUE — see the module comment.
pub struct PgPool
    options: pgconn.PgConnectOptions
    transport: pgconn.PgTransport
    policy: PgPoolPolicy
    // The connections not currently lent out, and when each last came
    // back.
    idle: [pgconn.PgConn]
    idle_since_ms: [Int]
    // How many are lent out.  A count rather than the connections
    // themselves, because a borrowed connection is the borrower's and
    // the pool has nothing to do with it until `release`.  Kept equal
    // to `list.len(lent_pids)`.
    lent: Int
    // How many times each IDLE connection has served, parallel to
    // `idle` — see `lent_pids` for the count while one is out.
    uses: [Int]
    // The lends in flight: for each one, the backend pid the handshake
    // gave the connection and the use count it went out with, so that
    // the release which matches the pid can give the count back.  A
    // connection's pid is unique among the live backends of a server,
    // which is exactly the set a pool holds; a connection that never
    // got a `BackendKeyData` — a handle built by hand — has pid zero,
    // and the first such lend is the one a release of one matches.
    lent_pids: [Int]
    lent_uses: [Int]
    // Counters a caller reports on.
    opened_total: Int
    closed_total: Int
    waits_total: Int
    timeouts_total: Int

// A pool and a connection taken from it.
pub struct PgAcquired
    pool: PgPool
    conn: pgconn.PgConn

// ── the pieces the bodies are built from ─────────────────────────────

// The clock in milliseconds, the unit every policy number is in.
//
// `warm` and `reap` take their own reading because neither has a
// caller-supplied one; every function that LENDS takes the caller's,
// so that a test can age a pool without sleeping.
fn now_of() -> Int [time]
    float.to_int(time.now() * 1000.0)

fn at_least(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
    if a > b
        a
    else
        b

fn at_most(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
    if a < b
        a
    else
        b

// The pool again with three counters MOVED TO — the absolute numbers,
// not deltas, so that a caller cannot double-count by composing two
// helpers.
fn counted(p: PgPool, opened: Int, closed: Int, waits: Int) -> PgPool
    PgPool { options: p.options, transport: p.transport, policy: p.policy,
             idle: p.idle, idle_since_ms: p.idle_since_ms, uses: p.uses,
             lent: p.lent, lent_pids: p.lent_pids, lent_uses: p.lent_uses,
             opened_total: opened, closed_total: closed, waits_total: waits,
             timeouts_total: p.timeouts_total }

// The pool again with its ledger and its idle list replaced.
fn relisted(p: PgPool, idle: [pgconn.PgConn], since: [Int], uses: [Int], lent: Int, pids: [Int],
            lent_uses: [Int]) -> PgPool
    PgPool { options: p.options, transport: p.transport, policy: p.policy,
             idle: idle, idle_since_ms: since, uses: uses,
             lent: lent, lent_pids: pids, lent_uses: lent_uses,
             opened_total: p.opened_total, closed_total: p.closed_total,
             waits_total: p.waits_total, timeouts_total: p.timeouts_total }

// A fresh copy of a list of ints, so that a caller can hand it on —
// a plain return of the list it was given is refused (E2038).
fn copy_ints(xs: [Int]) -> [Int]
    var out: [Int] = []
    for v in xs
        out = list.concat(out, [v])
    out

// One list without the element at `at`, and unchanged when `at` is
// below zero — which is what a lend the ledger does not know answers.
fn without_int(xs: [Int], at: Int) -> [Int]
    if at < 0
        return copy_ints(xs)
    list.concat(list.take(xs, at), list.drop(xs, at + 1))

// Whether the idle connection at `i` is one the pool will still lend:
// inside its idle ceiling and under its use count.  Pure, so that
// `has_idle` can ask it without either closing anything or declaring
// an effect it does not have.
fn live_at(p: PgPool, i: Int, now_ms: Int) -> Bool
    if p.policy.max_idle_ms > 0 and now_ms - p.idle_since_ms[i] > p.policy.max_idle_ms
        return false
    if p.policy.max_uses > 0 and p.uses[i] >= p.policy.max_uses
        return false
    true

// Close every idle connection and move the counter.
fn closed_all(p: PgPool) -> PgPool [io, net]
    var closed = p.closed_total
    for c in p.idle
        let _ = pgconn.close(c)
        closed = closed + 1
    counted(relisted(p, [], [], [], p.lent, p.lent_pids, p.lent_uses),
            p.opened_total, closed, p.waits_total)

// Close everything idle and answer the first fault, for the paths that
// are giving the pool up rather than answering it.
fn close_idle(p: PgPool) -> ?pgerror.PgFault [io, net]
    var first: ?pgerror.PgFault = None
    for c in p.idle
        match pgconn.close(c)
            None    => ()
            Some(f) =>
                if first == None
                    first = Some(f)
    first

// Where a lent connection sits in the ledger, or -1.
//
// The MATCH IS THE PID and not the position, because a pool is a
// value: two borrows can be released in the other order, and a
// release matched by position would then hand the wrong count back.
fn ledger_at(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn) -> Int
    var found = -1
    var i = 0
    while i < list.len(p.lent_pids) and found < 0
        if p.lent_pids[i] == c.process_id
            found = i
        i = i + 1
    found

// Hand a connection back into the idle list with the count it
// deserves, and take its lend out of the ledger.
fn kept(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn, now_ms: Int, served: Int) -> PgPool
    let at = ledger_at(p, c)
    counted(relisted(p, list.concat(p.idle, [c]),
                     list.concat(p.idle_since_ms, [now_ms]),
                     list.concat(p.uses, [served]),
                     at_least(p.lent - 1, 0),
                     without_int(p.lent_pids, at),
                     without_int(p.lent_uses, at)),
            p.opened_total, p.closed_total, p.waits_total)

// A connection that is not coming back at all: out of the ledger,
// counted closed, and the lend given up.
fn forgotten(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn, closed: Int) -> PgPool
    let at = ledger_at(p, c)
    counted(relisted(p, p.idle, p.idle_since_ms, p.uses,
                     at_least(p.lent - 1, 0),
                     without_int(p.lent_pids, at),
                     without_int(p.lent_uses, at)),
            p.opened_total, p.closed_total + closed, p.waits_total)

// The count a returning connection was lent with.  A connection the
// ledger does not know — one the caller kept a copy of after a release
// that failed, say — is taken as having served once, which is the
// count a connection returns with on its first lend.
fn served_count(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn) -> Int
    let at = ledger_at(p, c)
    if at < 0
        return 1
    p.lent_uses[at]

// Open one connection, run the policy's `on_connect_sql` on it, and
// answer it.
//
// A failure anywhere closes what was opened before answering: a
// connection the pool cannot use is not left open, because a dropped
// `PgConn` does not close its socket.
fn open_one(p: PgPool) -> Result<pgconn.PgConn, pgerror.PgFault> [io, net, time]
    let c = pgconn.connect(p.options, p.transport)!
    if str.len(p.policy.on_connect_sql) == 0
        return Ok(c)
    let sent = match pgconn.send(c, pgmsg.PgQuery(sql: p.policy.on_connect_sql))
        Err(f) =>
            let _ = pgconn.close(c)
            return Err(f)
        Ok(v)  => v
    match run_to_boundary(sent)
        Err(f)   =>
            let _ = pgconn.close(c)
            Err(f)
        Ok(done) => Ok(done)

// Read to `ReadyForQuery`, answering the server's error if it sent one.
//
// `pgconn.drain_to_ready` reads to the boundary and cannot fail a
// statement — an `ErrorResponse` is a message like any other to it —
// which is the wrong answer for `on_connect_sql`, where a `SET` that
// did not take must not be reported as a pool that is ready.
fn run_to_boundary(c: pgconn.PgConn) -> Result<pgconn.PgConn, pgerror.PgFault> [io, net, time]
    var conn = c
    var done = false
    var fault: ?pgerror.PgFault = None
    while not done
        let r = pgconn.next_message(conn)!
        conn = r.conn
        match r.message
            PgErrorResponse(fields) =>
                if fault == None
                    fault = Some(pgerror.PgServerError(fields: fields))
            _                       => ()
        if pgmsg.ends_exchange(r.message)
            done = true
    match fault
        Some(f) => Err(f)
        None    => Ok(conn)

// ── the pool ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A sensible policy: ten connections, none kept idle, a five-second
// wait, a five-minute idle ceiling, reset on release.
//
// `max_uses` is zero — never recycle for age of use — because a
// program that wants a prepared-statement ceiling has to name the
// number it wants.
//
// ```novo
// use pgpool
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pgpool.default_policy().max_connections}")
// ```
pub fn default_policy() -> PgPoolPolicy
    PgPoolPolicy { max_connections: 10, min_connections: 0, wait_ms: 5000,
                   max_idle_ms: 300000, max_uses: 0, reset_on_release: true,
                   on_connect_sql: "" }

// An empty pool.
//
// Opens nothing: `min_connections` is honoured by `warm`, so that a
// program can decide whether the handshakes happen at start-up or on
// the first request.
//
// ```novo
// use pgpool
// use pgconn
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pgpool.new_pool(pgconn.default_options("postgres"),
//                             pgconn.plain_transport(), pgpool.default_policy())
//     println("${p.lent}")
// ```
pub fn new_pool(o: pgconn.PgConnectOptions, t: pgconn.PgTransport, policy: PgPoolPolicy) -> PgPool
    PgPool { options: o, transport: t, policy: policy,
             idle: [], idle_since_ms: [], uses: [], lent: 0,
             lent_pids: [], lent_uses: [],
             opened_total: 0, closed_total: 0,
             waits_total: 0, timeouts_total: 0 }

// Open connections for start-up, and prove the pool can open one.
//
// So that the handshakes — a TCP connect, a TLS handshake and four
// thousand rounds of SCRAM each — happen at start-up rather than on
// the first request, which is when somebody is watching.
//
// THE FIRST CONNECTION IS OPENED EVEN WHEN `min_connections` IS ZERO,
// and that is the point of the call rather than an oversight: a pool
// that has never opened anything has proved nothing, and a start-up
// that reported success because there was nothing to do would move
// the first failure to the first request — the timing this call
// exists to avoid.  So a warm pool that cannot reach the server is a
// refusal with the fault, and the connections opened before the
// failure are closed before it is answered.
//
// A `min_connections` above `max_connections` is capped rather than
// refused: the limit is the limit.
pub fn warm(p: PgPool) -> Result<PgPool, pgerror.PgFault> [io, net, time]
    let target = at_most(at_least(p.policy.min_connections, 1),
                         p.policy.max_connections)
    var pool = p
    while pool.lent + list.len(pool.idle) < target
        let attempt = open_one(pool)
        match attempt
            Err(f) =>
                let _ = closed_all(pool)
                return Err(f)
            Ok(c)  =>
                pool = opened_into(pool, c, now_of())
    Ok(pool)

// One more idle connection, counted as opened.
fn opened_into(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn, now_ms: Int) -> PgPool
    counted(relisted(p, list.concat(p.idle, [c]),
                     list.concat(p.idle_since_ms, [now_ms]),
                     list.concat(p.uses, [0]),
                     p.lent, p.lent_pids, p.lent_uses),
            p.opened_total + 1, p.closed_total, p.waits_total)

// Close every idle connection that is past its idle ceiling or its use
// count.
fn sweep(p: PgPool, now_ms: Int) -> PgPool [io, net]
    var kept_conns: [pgconn.PgConn] = []
    var since: [Int] = []
    var took: [Int] = []
    var closed = p.closed_total
    for i in 0..list.len(p.idle)
        if live_at(p, i, now_ms)
            kept_conns = list.concat(kept_conns, [p.idle[i]])
            since = list.concat(since, [p.idle_since_ms[i]])
            took = list.concat(took, [p.uses[i]])
        else
            let _ = pgconn.close(p.idle[i])
            closed = closed + 1
    counted(relisted(p, kept_conns, since, took, p.lent, p.lent_pids,
                     p.lent_uses),
            p.opened_total, closed, p.waits_total)

// Take a connection.
//
// Lends an idle one that is still good, opens a new one if the limit
// allows, or answers `PgPoolExhausted` carrying both numbers.
//
// IT DOES NOT WAIT, whatever `wait_ms` says, and the reason is in the
// module comment: with a pool that is a value there is no other
// writer, so a wait would be a loop against a clock that changes
// nothing.  A `wait_ms` of zero — the default for a handler with a
// deadline of its own — and a `wait_ms` of five seconds therefore
// behave the same here, and a caller that must wait retries on its
// own deadline against a pool it owns through a `cell`.
//
// `now_ms` is the CALLER's clock reading, so that a test can age a
// pool without sleeping and so that a program with one clock read per
// request does not take another here.
//
// ```novo
// use pgpool
// use pgconn
//
// fn main() [io, net, time]
//     let p = pgpool.new_pool(pgconn.default_options("postgres"),
//                             pgconn.plain_transport(), pgpool.default_policy())
//     match pgpool.acquire(p, 0)
//         Ok(a)  => println("${a.pool.lent}")
//         Err(_) => println("exhausted")
// ```
pub fn acquire(p: PgPool, now_ms: Int) -> Result<PgAcquired, pgerror.PgFault> [io, net, time]
    let ready = sweep(p, now_ms)
    if list.len(ready.idle) > 0
        return Ok(lend(ready))
    if ready.lent + list.len(ready.idle) >= ready.policy.max_connections
        return Err(pgerror.PgPoolExhausted(in_use: ready.lent,
                                           limit: ready.policy.max_connections))
    let attempt = open_one(ready)
    match attempt
        Err(f) =>
            let _ = closed_all(ready)
            Err(f)
        Ok(c)  =>
            let grown = counted(opened_into(ready, c, now_ms),
                                ready.opened_total + 1, ready.closed_total,
                                ready.waits_total + 1)
            Ok(lend(grown))

// Lend the head of the idle list, recording the lend in the ledger.
fn lend(p: PgPool) -> PgAcquired
    let c = p.idle[0]
    PgAcquired { pool: relisted(p, list.drop(p.idle, 1),
                                list.drop(p.idle_since_ms, 1),
                                list.drop(p.uses, 1),
                                p.lent + 1,
                                list.concat(p.lent_pids, [c.process_id]),
                                list.concat(p.lent_uses, [p.uses[0] + 1])),
                 conn: c }

// True when the connection is inside a transaction the next borrower
// must not inherit.
fn needs_reset(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn) -> Bool
    if not p.policy.reset_on_release
        return false
    match c.status
        PgInTransaction       => true
        PgInFailedTransaction => true
        _                     => false

// Give a connection back.
//
// THE OPERATION THAT MATTERS — see the module comment.  Checks the
// transaction status and, when `reset_on_release` is set, rolls back
// rather than trusting the borrower; a connection left in
// `PgInFailedTransaction` or `PgInTransaction` would otherwise hand
// the next borrower somebody else's transaction.  A connection that
// is broken, too old or too used is closed instead of kept.
//
// A CLOSE ON THE WAY BACK IS A SUCCESSFUL RELEASE: a broken
// connection, and one whose rollback failed, are closed and counted in
// `closed_total`, and the pool is answered — there is nothing for the
// caller to do about a socket that is already gone.
//
// THE ONE `Err` IS A ROLLBACK THAT FAILED.  The connection is closed
// — never kept, because its transaction state is now unknown — and
// the fault is answered because a caller must hear that a statement
// it thought had rolled back may not have.  THE POOL THE CALLER STILL
// HOLDS STILL COUNTS THAT LEND: an `Err` carries no pool, so there is
// nowhere to give the slot back, and a caller that kept a copy of the
// connection can free it with `discard`.  That is the trade the value
// shape makes and it is written here rather than discovered.
//
// ```novo
// use pgpool
// use pgconn
//
// fn main() [io, net, time]
//     let p = pgpool.new_pool(pgconn.default_options("postgres"),
//                             pgconn.plain_transport(), pgpool.default_policy())
//     match pgpool.acquire(p, 0)
//         Err(_) => println("exhausted")
//         Ok(a)  =>
//             match pgpool.release(a.pool, a.conn, 0)
//                 Ok(p2) => println("${p2.lent}")
//                 Err(_) => println("could not reset")
// ```
pub fn release(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn,
               now_ms: Int) -> Result<PgPool, pgerror.PgFault> [io, net, time]
    let served = served_count(p, c)
    if c.broken
        let _ = pgconn.close(c)
        return Ok(forgotten(p, c, 1))
    if not needs_reset(p, c)
        return Ok(kept(p, c, now_ms, served))
    match pgquery.rollback(c)
        Err(f)    =>
            let _ = pgconn.close(c)
            Err(f)
        // The connection the rollback answered is the one kept: it is
        // the one whose `status` says the transaction is over, and a
        // borrower that read the stale one would think otherwise.
        Ok(reset) => Ok(kept(p, reset, now_ms, served))

// Give a connection back and throw it away.
//
// What a caller does when it knows the connection is no good — it
// cancelled a query on it, or it caught a `pgerror.is_fatal` — rather
// than making the pool work it out.
pub fn discard(p: PgPool, c: pgconn.PgConn) -> PgPool [io, net]
    let _ = pgconn.close(c)
    forgotten(p, c, 1)

// Close every idle connection older than `max_idle_ms`, and top back
// up to `min_connections`.
//
// Called by a program's own timer; this package starts no thread and
// has no timer of its own, which is what keeps it a value.
//
// `min_connections` here is honoured as written: a fresh pool that
// has lost everything is refilled, but a pool that never had a
// minimum is not obliged to prove anything — that is `warm`'s job,
// and doing it again on every reap would open a connection on a timer
// forever.
pub fn reap(p: PgPool, now_ms: Int) -> Result<PgPool, pgerror.PgFault> [io, net, time]
    let swept = sweep(p, now_ms)
    let target = at_most(swept.policy.min_connections, swept.policy.max_connections)
    var pool = swept
    while pool.lent + list.len(pool.idle) < target
        let attempt = open_one(pool)
        match attempt
            Err(f) =>
                let _ = closed_all(pool)
                return Err(f)
            Ok(c)  =>
                pool = opened_into(pool, c, now_ms)
    Ok(pool)

// Close everything the pool holds.
//
// THE VALUE THE CALLER HOLDS IS NOT THE THING THAT WAS CLOSED, and
// this function has to be told so: the sockets are closed, and the
// `PgPool` in the caller's hand still lists them, so a pool that has
// been through here must not be used again.  It answers the first
// close that failed, or `None`.
//
// LENT CONNECTIONS ARE NOT CLOSED.  The pool does not hold them —
// that is what lending means — and a close that reached for one would
// be a close of somebody else's socket.
pub fn close_pool(p: PgPool) -> ?pgerror.PgFault [io, net]
    close_idle(p)

// ── what a caller reports on ─────────────────────────────────────────

// A pool's counters, for a metrics endpoint.
pub struct PgPoolStats
    idle: Int
    lent: Int
    limit: Int
    opened_total: Int
    closed_total: Int
    // How many `acquire` calls had to wait, and how many gave up.
    // The pair that says whether the limit is too low: waits without
    // timeouts is a pool at capacity and coping, waits WITH timeouts
    // is a pool that is losing requests.
    //
    // `waits_total` counts the acquires that could not be served from
    // an idle connection — the handshake was on the critical path —
    // and `timeouts_total` STAYS ZERO for a pool this package built:
    // a refusal is an `Err`, an `Err` carries no pool, and there is
    // nowhere to record it.  See the module comment.  A caller that
    // wants the second number counts its own `PgPoolExhausted`s.
    waits_total: Int
    timeouts_total: Int

// The counters.
//
// ```novo
// use pgpool
// use pgconn
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pgpool.new_pool(pgconn.default_options("postgres"),
//                             pgconn.plain_transport(), pgpool.default_policy())
//     println("${pgpool.stats(p).limit}")
// ```
pub fn stats(p: PgPool) -> PgPoolStats
    PgPoolStats { idle: list.len(p.idle), lent: p.lent,
                  limit: p.policy.max_connections,
                  opened_total: p.opened_total, closed_total: p.closed_total,
                  waits_total: p.waits_total,
                  timeouts_total: p.timeouts_total }

// Whether the pool would lend right now without opening.
//
// The question a load shedder asks before it accepts a request.
//
// It COUNTS the same connections `acquire` would lend — the ones that
// are neither past the idle ceiling nor past their use count — and it
// does not close the ones that are: closing is not a question a load
// shedder asks, and a pure predicate is one a caller can call in the
// middle of anything.
pub fn has_idle(p: PgPool, now_ms: Int) -> Bool
    var i = 0
    var found = false
    while i < list.len(p.idle) and not found
        if live_at(p, i, now_ms)
            found = true
        i = i + 1
    found
